<markdown_cell>
# Comparing ShellCheck word-splitting profiles: SC2086, SC2068, and SC2206

This notebook compares three ShellCheck rules that all flag unquoted expansions but trigger in different contexts: scalar variables (SC2086), array expansions with `[@]` (SC2068), and array-assignment from an unquoted expansion (SC2206). Each section shows a trigger, the fix, and the suppression pattern used in production scripts.

> **Scope** — ShellCheck 0.10.0 on Linux. Bash 5.x. All code cells are self-contained snippets; each is tagged with the rule it demonstrates or suppresses.
> **last_verified:** 2026-09-09  
> **tool_version:** shellcheck 0.10.0  
> **sources:** n/a
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Purpose

In a Bash production script, an unquoted variable expansion silently invites two hazards: word splitting (the value is broken on `IFS` whitespace) and pathname expansion (glob characters `*`, `?`, `[` are expanded against the filesystem). ShellCheck encodes these as a family of rules with adjacent numbers. This notebook clarifies which rule fires where, because the suppression idiom differs for each and CI gates often treat the severities differently.
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## When to use

| Scenario | Action |
|---|---|
| CI gate runs `shellcheck -S error` | Only SC2068 will fail the gate; SC2086 and SC2206 pass silently. Verify separately. |
| CI gate runs `shellcheck -S warning` | All three rules will fail; triage each by context. |
| Code review flagged one of these rules | Use this notebook to determine whether the warning is a genuine bug or intentional word splitting. |
| Adding `--exclude` to `.shellcheckrc` | See the Suppression strategies section for rule-specific caveats. |
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Prerequisites

- **ShellCheck >= 0.6.0** — rule numbering for SC2086/SC2068/SC2206 is stable; none have changed since 0.4.x.
- **Bash 4.3+** — the rules apply regardless of Bash version; suppression syntax (`# shellcheck disable=`) is unchanged since ShellCheck 0.3.
- **`shellcheck` on `PATH`** — or install via `apt install shellcheck`, `brew install shellcheck`, or download the static binary.
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Key terminology

- **SC2086** — ShellCheck rule *Double quote to prevent globbing and word splitting*. Fires on unquoted scalar variable expansions in command position (e.g. `echo $var`). Severity: `info`.
- **SC2068** — ShellCheck rule *Double quote to prevent globbing and word splitting*. Array-expansion variant of SC2086; fires on unquoted `${arr[@]}`, `${arr[*]}`. Severity: `error`.
- **SC2206** — ShellCheck rule *Quote to prevent word splitting/globbing, or split robustly with mapfile or read -a*. Fires on array assignment from an unquoted expansion (e.g. `split=($var)`). Severity: `warning`.
- **Word splitting** — The shell re-splits the result of an unquoted expansion into multiple fields using `IFS` (default: space, tab, newline).
- **Pathname expansion (globbing)** — After word splitting, the shell expands `*` `?` `[...]` patterns to matching filenames.
- **Inline suppression** — `# shellcheck disable=SCxxxx` placed on the line above the offender.
- **CLI exclusion** — `--exclude=SCxxxx` or `-e SCxxxx` passed to the `shellcheck` command; applies to the entire file or invocation.
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## SC2086 — unquoted scalar variable

`echo $var` and `rm -f $file` trigger SC2086. ShellCheck suggests quoting the variable to prevent both word splitting and globbing.
</markdown_cell>

<code_cell>
#!/usr/bin/env bash

# sc2086-trigger.sh — demonstrate SC2086 (info)
# Running shellcheck on this file reports SC2086 on both lines.

var="hello world"
echo $var        # SC2086: unquoted scalar — word-splits into "hello" "world"
rm -f $var       # SC2086: unquoted scalar — glob expansion risk
</code_cell>

<markdown_cell>
The fix wraps each expansion in double quotes, which disables word splitting and prevents globbing:
</markdown_cell>

<code_cell>
#!/usr/bin/env bash

# sc2086-fix.sh — quote every scalar expansion
var="hello world"
echo "$var"
rm -f "$var"
</code_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## SC2068 — unquoted array expansion

When the variable is an array, `${arr[@]}` or `${arr[*]}` without surrounding quotes triggers SC2068 rather than SC2086. ShellCheck suggests `"${arr[@]}"`.
</markdown_cell>

<code_cell>
#!/usr/bin/env bash

# sc2068-trigger.sh — demonstrate SC2068 (error)
# Running shellcheck on this file reports SC2068 on the echo line.
# Note: severity is 'error', so it fails even -S error gates.

arr=(one "two three")
echo ${arr[@]}      # SC2068: array elements re-split on whitespace
</code_cell>

<markdown_cell>
`echo ${arr[@]}` (unquoted) passes each element through a second round of word splitting, so `"two three"` becomes two words. `"${arr[@]}"` (quoted) preserves each element as a single argument. ShellCheck classifies this as SC2068 with severity `error`, making it the most severe of the three rules.
</markdown_cell>

<code_cell>
#!/usr/bin/env bash

# sc2068-fix.sh — quote array expansions
arr=(one "two three")
echo "${arr[@]}"
</code_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## SC2206 — array assignment from unquoted expansion

`split=($var)` triggers SC2206. This is the most dangerous of the three because it creates an array from a value that may have been intended as a single string. ShellCheck suggests quoting (`single=("$var")`) or splitting robustly with `mapfile` or `read -ra`.
</markdown_cell>

<code_cell>
#!/usr/bin/env bash

# sc2206-trigger.sh — demonstrate SC2206 (warning)
# Running shellcheck on this file reports SC2206 on the assignment line.

var="alpha beta gamma"
split=($var)        # SC2206: word-splits "alpha beta gamma" into 3 elements
echo "${split[0]}"  # only "alpha" — the rest may have been unintended
</code_cell>

<markdown_cell>
Without quotes, `split=($var)` word-splits `"alpha beta gamma"` into three array elements. This is usually a bug: if the intent was a single-element array, the code silently creates three. If word splitting is the actual intent, the code should use `read -ra` or `mapfile` for clarity.
</markdown_cell>

<code_cell>
#!/usr/bin/env bash

# sc2206-fix.sh — either preserve as one element or split explicitly
var="alpha beta gamma"

# Single-element array (preserve the entire string as one element):
single=("$var")
echo "${single[0]}"

# Intentional word split — use read -ra for clarity:
read -ra parts <<< "$var"
echo "${parts[0]}"
</code_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Rule comparison

| Rule | Expansion form | Severity | What ShellCheck detects | Canonical fix |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| SC2086 | `$var`, `${var}` | info | Unquoted scalar expansion in command position | `"$var"` |
| SC2068 | `${arr[@]}`, `${arr[*]}` | error | Array expansion re-split into words | `"${arr[@]}"` |
| SC2206 | `arr=($var)` | warning | Array assignment word-splits the RHS | `"{$var}"` or `read -ra` / `mapfile` |

**Severity ordering matters for CI gating:** SC2068 is treated as `error` (fails `-S error`), SC2206 as `warning`, and SC2086 as `info`. A gate using `-S error` will flag only SC2068; a gate using `-S warning` catches all three.
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Suppression strategies

### Inline: `# shellcheck disable=`

The most precise suppression. Place the directive on the line immediately above the offending line. Multiple rules can be listed comma-separated. Each suppression should be followed by a brief `# reason:` comment in production code.
</markdown_cell>

<code_cell>
#!/usr/bin/env bash

# sc-suppress-inline.sh — inline suppressions for all three rules
var="hello world"
arr=(one two)

# Intentional word splitting in a for-loop:
# shellcheck disable=SC2086
for word in $var; do
    echo "$word"
done

# Intentional: iterate over pre-split array elements:
# shellcheck disable=SC2068
echo ${arr[@]}

# Intentional: word-split into an array:
# shellcheck disable=SC2206
split=($var)
echo "${split[0]}"
</code_cell>

<markdown_cell>
Note: the `for word in $var` pattern does **not** trigger SC2086 without a suppression directive, because ShellCheck recognises that unquoted iteration is idiomatic. The inline `# shellcheck disable=SC2086` is shown for completeness and to document intent explicitly.
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
### CLI: `--exclude` / `-e`

File-level or project-level exclusion is less precise but useful for CI jobs that scan many files with a shared `.shellcheckrc`. The `--exclude` flag is deprecated in favour of `-e`, but both are accepted by ShellCheck 0.10.0.
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
```
# Exclude all three rules for a single file:
shellcheck --exclude=SC2086,SC2068,SC2206 script.sh

# Or use -e (preferred; one rule per flag):
shellcheck -e SC2086 -e SC2068 -e SC2206 script.sh

# Project-wide: write a .shellcheckrc file:
# exclude=SC2086,SC2068,SC2206
```
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
### When suppression is appropriate

| Rule | Suppress when | Never suppress |
|---|---|---|
| SC2086 | Intentional word splitting (`for word in $var`) or variable is a hardcoded literal with no spaces/globs. | Variable comes from user input, config files, or `find` output. |
| SC2068 | Rare — only when array elements are explicitly meant to be re-split (uncommon). | Default: always quote `"${arr[@]}"`. |
| SC2206 | Never — if word splitting is intended, use `read -ra` or `mapfile` explicitly. | Always — if word splitting is intended, use `read -ra` or `mapfile` explicitly. |
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Verify

1. **SC2086:** `shellcheck -e SC2086 sc2086-trigger.sh` should report the two unquoted scalar lines.
2. **SC2068:** `shellcheck -e SC2068 sc2068-trigger.sh` should report the unquoted array expansion.
3. **SC2206:** `shellcheck -e SC2206 sc2206-trigger.sh` should report the array assignment.
4. **Fix verification:** `shellcheck sc2086-fix.sh sc2068-fix.sh sc2206-fix.sh` should exit `0` (no warnings).
5. **Inline suppression:** `shellcheck sc-suppress-inline.sh` should exit `0`.
6. **CLI exclusion:** `shellcheck --exclude=SC2086,SC2068,SC2206 sc2086-trigger.sh sc2068-trigger.sh sc2206-trigger.sh` should exit `0`.
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Common errors

- **Quoting only some, not all.** Mixing `"${arr[@]}"` (correct) with `${arr[@]}` (SC2068) in the same script leaves the warning active. The fix is binary: quote every array expansion or none — partial quoting is a common code-review failure.
- **Using `$arr` instead of `${arr[@]}` or `$arr[0]`** when only the first element is wanted. `$arr` on a declared array triggers SC2128 (Expanding an array without an index only gives the first element), which is a separate warning outside this rule set but commonly confused with SC2206.
- **`--exclude` in CI silences everything.** A blanket `shellcheck --exclude=SC2086,SC2068,SC2206` in CI hides genuine regressions. Prefer per-line inline disables with `# reason:` comments so the intent travels with the code.
- **`read -ra` without `IFS=` read.** `read -ra split <<< "$var"` splits on default `IFS` (spaces, tabs, newlines). If the value contains tabs and only spaces are intended as delimiters, set `IFS=' '` explicitly: `IFS=' ' read -ra split <<< "$var"`.
- **Confusing SC2086 with SC2068 severity.** Because SC2086 is `info` and SC2068 is `error`, a team that runs `shellcheck -S error` will miss unquoted scalar variables entirely, creating a false sense of safety.
</markdown_cell>

